 { Let's see a practical ending where Black used the 'one pawn stops two' idea to win the game. } 1... { -KEY- } d4 $1 ( { The move order matters: the immediate advance } 1... h5 $2 { allows White to save himself with } 2. g4 $1 hxg4 3. h5 { and both players queen. } ) 
 { After this move, White resigned; let's see a possible continuation: } 2. Kd1 h5 $1 { One pawn stops two. } ( { In contrast, } 2... Kd3 $2 { would only draw after } 3. g4 { , since Black is left with a rook's pawn in the end. } ) 
3. Kc1 ( { Or } 3. g4 hxg4 4. h5 g3 { and Black queens with check - Baburin. } ) 
d3 4. Kd1 d2 5. g3 Kd3 { Black wins. } 
 0-1

