 { In a typical Catalan endgame, Levenfish probably didn't think twice about his next move. } 1. { -KEY- } a5 $1 { An advance with several virtues: Black's pawn majority is paralysed, the strong knight on c5 is secured, and the black bishop remains tied to the b7-pawn. Any other move would have permitted the freeing 1...b6. } Ke7 ( { The attempt to exchange the annoying enemy knight with } 1... Ne6 { is well met by } 2. f5 $1 { , when } Nxc5 3. bxc5 { , followed by Bc4, Kf4, e4-e5, g3-g4, h2-h4 and g4-g5 should win for White. } ) 
2. Bc4 Kd6 3. Kd4 Ne8 4. e5+ { Unlike his opponent, White has an easy task in advancing his own pawn majority. } fxe5+ 5. fxe5+ Ke7 6. h4 $1 { Levenfish has observed that Black has no solid move at his disposal, and puts him into some kind of zugzwang. } ( { In contrast, the more straightforward } 6. Ne4 { , preparing Kc5, permits } Bf5 ) 
Nc7 ( { Or } 6... g5 7. hxg5 hxg5 8. Ne4 g4 9. Ke3 { , followed by Kf4, winning the g4-pawn - Shereshevsky. } ) 
7. Ne4 Be6 { Now the b7-pawn falls, but Black had no good alternative; } ( { a) e.g. } 7... Bf5 8. Nd6 ) 
 ( { b) or } 7... Ne8 8. Kc5 { , followed by Kb6 and Nc5. } ) 
8. Nd6 Bxc4 9. Kxc4 Ke6 10. Nxb7 ( { Black resigned, in view of } 10. Nxb7 Kxe5 11. Kc5 Nd5 12. Nd8 Ke4 13. Nxc6 Nc3 14. Nb8 Kf3 15. Nxa6 Kxg3 16. Nc7 { - Shereshevsky. } ) 

 1-0
